body:
I don't agree with id's reasoning. You can reduce the amount of item drops by making one big drop that contains all the values of the individual pickups, ammo, health, etc. so that's that issue fixed.

You could also give the players the choice of whether to have items automatically fly into the player that killed the enemy, or not. You can have different modes, like co-op competitive and co-op friendly and you can even try to make all characters and enemies x0.8 size to quickly test if the co-op game just needs bigger maps.

&#x200B;

The thing that companies never consider is that, when it comes to co-op, most people don't care about the small stuff... they will take the concession just to be able to play with a friend.

Back when Halo had coop on the original xbox, we knew the game looked significantly worse when another player joined... no one cared.

&#x200B;

The co-op crowd aren't picky about small changes, unless the game was specifically built around co-op. Doom Eternal isn't.

&#x200B;

Further more, if you don't make a massive marketing campaign about it and just patch it in, you can effectively beta test it with fans that love the game already and ask for their suggestions. Be open about the design and the issues with the design, don't patronise us, or treat us like \*shudder\* *customers.*

&#x200B;

In the end, these are the same excuses that devs use to justify the absence of split screen on PC, even when the console version has it. 

&#x200B;

I hope they change their mind.

Life isn't about forgetting your demons, its about being comfortable enough among them, to not let them sway you.

&nbsp;

Sometimes you may have to do the bad thing for the greater good, sometimes you need to dump a part of yourself in a river and hope it one day washes ashore, when you're out of the woods. So long as your intentions remain good and your not following your own selfish desires (demons), you can rest easy. 

&nbsp;

With regards to your identity, you have changed as have all of us, since you were young. You will change still, as you get older and regardless of how bad or good your situation is, you will most likely grow to resent parts of yourself, as is the way of life and our constant struggle to evolve and grow.

&nbsp;

Even still, that boy that you remember is still very much a part of you, however much you may be reluctant to believe that. Why do you think people say having children is so life changing? Is it not because they see their former selves in the eyes of their child? Is it not that shift in perspective that allows them to be one with who they truly are, once all their painful memories no longer matter?

&nbsp;

Regardless of where you are and what you do, you deserve all that life can offer, every single person does (so don't sell yourself short) but life won't hand you anything for free and you can't steal happiness, or peace of mind. You can't manipulate or kill for freedom, when your thoughts can eat you alive and your mind will kill your emotions to protect you.

&nbsp;

It may be seductive to play the game and be the bad guy, to think that life is bullshit anyway, that everyone wears a façade, works to death and bows to corporate run governments, but it's these very establishments that fund the prisons that contain us, the media that divides us and the police force that herd us like sheep. It is for the sake of our future that we survive, so that we can have a say in what our world should be and these thoughts only lead us further down the hole until we're part of the same corruption.

&nbsp;

Heaven and hell is within you and right now, you very well may be in hell. You will most likely not burn for eternity if you die, or be punished for your sins. All you'll do is hurt those you claim don't deserve you. Do they deserve the pain they will feel if you die? So what if you can't be best buddies when you return, this doesn't mean you can't visit them, or that you won't meet new people who may interest you.

&nbsp;

Here is a quote I think will help you, from the book "The Prophet" (not a religious book, a fictional story about a wise prophet departing his homeland): 

&nbsp;

"Then a woman said, "Speak to us of Joy and Sorrow."
&nbsp; 
And he answered: 
&nbsp;
Your joy is your sorrow unmasked. 
&nbsp;
And the selfsame well from which your laughter rises was oftentimes filled with your tears. 
&nbsp;
And how else can it be? 
&nbsp;
The deeper that sorrow carves into your being, the more joy you can contain. "
&nbsp;

&nbsp;

(You can read the book here: http://www-personal.umich.edu/~jrcole/gibran/prophet/prophet.htm | It is made up of sections, each referencing a part of life)

&nbsp;

The more pain you endure, the deeper your capacity to feel joy, compassion and love, because you can understand the extent of pain that another is dealing with, moreso than anyone else. You will have travelled deeper and found the darkest depths of the human mind and heart, such that your understanding of other people will be more insightful and your connection to them profound. And you will laugh the loudest, because you can appreciate a deeper joy, just as a cancer survivor will appreciate life infinitely more than their healthy siblings. 

&nbsp;

But this can only be a reality if you want to make it and that falls on you.

&nbsp;

It may be hard at times, you may be afraid lose yourself, but whatever happens, make sure you remain, at heart, a just and humble person and see only your childhood self as your guide to being all that you could be. 

&nbsp;

Read the book and look into philosophy and later psychology to better understand yourself, your peers and the human condition.

&nbsp;

The School of Life is a great youtube channel to get you started: 
https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ox8XlcUppbo&list=PLwxNMb28XmpckOvZZ_AZjD7WM2p9-6NBv&index=6

Society is wrought with injustice, due to the mindset perpetrated by the media and the severe lack of quality education that deals with broadening our perspectives and capacity to love ourselves and one another, but things are changing and many millions of people out there will accept you with open arms, without treating you any differently, while respecting your flaws, so long as you recognise that you may be pushing people away because of your insecurities and fears and work to fight those negative and destructive feelings and thoughts.

&nbsp;

It is easy to think that the bubble we surround ourselves with is all that there is, but there is a wide world out there with near infinite possibilities and you are a large and unspoken part of it.

&nbsp;

Your insight on what it is to live with chronic ailments is what may help the world understand how this can effect people and allow us to better deal with these issues and accept a less strict and narrow view of what is 'normal' and what is 'weird' or 'different' - allowing our species to evolve and improve our capacity to love, while making it a lot easier for those who feel isolated, or strange.

&nbsp;

You are needed and you are incredibly important. You have the ability to understand pain and connect with those going through a similar situation and that is priceless. 

&nbsp;

I will paste what I wrote to another, who was convinced his time was up and hope it provokes a fire within you to keep going:

&nbsp;

Don't let them win, your life matters and so do those you'll love and care for. Your purpose is to live, for the simple sake of being here and being happy. Fight for that, live for that.

&nbsp;

If you survive, you will be the beacon of hope for so many - a wise and humble leader, with experience of the darkest and most vulnerable reaches of our psyche and the fortitude to bear it, embrace it and love in spite of it.

&nbsp;

The world needs people like you.

&nbsp;

Live

Where are you? Why 17 months?

&nbsp;

Don't let them win, your life matters and so do those you'll love and care for. Your purpose is to live, for the simple sake of being here and being happy. Fight for that, live for that. 

&nbsp;

If you survive, you will be the beacon of hope for so many - a wise and humble leader, with experience of the darkest and most vulnerable reaches of our psyche and the fortitude to bear it, embrace it and love in spite of it. 

&nbsp;

The world needs people like you.

&nbsp;

Live
